Below is a compact console‑application that demonstrates a few core LINQ operations (filtering, ordering, projection, and grouping).  
```csharp
using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;
using System.Linq;

namespace LinqDemo
{
    // Simple POCO that will be our query data source
    class Person
    {
        public string FirstName { get; set; }
        public string LastName  { get; set; }
        public int    Age       { get; set; }
        public string City      { get; set; }
    }

    class Program
    {
        static void Main()
        {
            // Seed a list of people
            var people = new List<Person>
            {
                new Person { FirstName = "Alice",   LastName = "Smith",  Age = 30, City = "New York" },
                new Person { FirstName = "Bob",     LastName = "Jones",  Age = 17, City = "Chicago" },
                new Person { FirstName = "Charlie", LastName = "Brown",  Age = 25, City = "New York" },
                new Person { FirstName = "Dana",    LastName = "Davis",  Age = 22, City = "Los Angeles" },
                new Person { FirstName = "Eli",     LastName = "Evans",  Age = 19, City = "Chicago" },
                new Person { FirstName = "Fiona",   LastName = "Foster", Age = 35, City = "Los Angeles" }
            };

            // 1. Filtering (Where) & ordering (OrderBy / ThenBy)
            var adults = people
                .Where(p => p.Age >= 18)                // keep only adults
                .OrderBy(p => p.LastName)               // primary key: last name
                .ThenBy(p => p.FirstName);              // secondary key: first name

            Console.WriteLine("Adults (sorted by name):");
            foreach (var p in adults)
                Console.WriteLine($"{p.FirstName} {p.LastName}, Age {p.Age}, City {p.City}");
            Console.WriteLine();

            // 2. Projection (Select) – extract just the full names
            var fullNames = people
                .Select(p => $"{p.FirstName} {p.LastName}");

            Console.WriteLine("All people – full names:");
            foreach (var name in fullNames)
                Console.WriteLine(name);
            Console.WriteLine();

            // 3. Grouping (GroupBy) – count how many people live in each city
            var peopleByCity = people
                .GroupBy(p => p.City)
                .Select(g => new { City = g.Key, Count = g.Count() });

            Console.WriteLine("People count per city:");
            foreach (var group in peopleByCity)
                Console.WriteLine($"{group.City}: {group.Count}");
        }
    }
}
```

### What the code does

1. **Filter** the list for people 18 + using `Where`.  
2. **Order** the filtered list by last name, then first name.  
3. **Select** (project) each person to a string that contains their full name.  
4. **Group** the original list by `City` and count how many people are in each group.  

Run the program and you’ll see each operation’s result printed to the console. This showcases LINQ’s expressive, query‑like syntax for working with in‑memory collections.
